  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 311 AM EDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nMoving into Wednesday, ridging over the western CONUS will broaden \nand approach the mid-Mississippi Valley region. This will increase \ntemperatures back to near normal in the upper 80s. At the surface, \nhigh pressure will push off to the east and a warm front will move \ninto the lower Ohio Valley. This front will bring increased moisture \nand lift to broad upper troughing. Looking to see isolated to \nscattered chances for showers and storms on Wednesday over southern \nand central Kentucky. Forecast soundings show PWATs around 1.7-1.8 \ninches, weak shear, and ample instability Wednesday afternoon. \nThough, mid-level lapse rates look poor. Given this environment, we \ncould see a strong pulse storm or two, but severe weather is not \nexpected at this time.\n\nOn Thursday, a shortwave will swing through the Great Lakes region \nand bring a weak cold front through the Ohio Valley. Current timing \nof the front is Thursday into Friday. Given the antecedent \nenvironment, would expected to see scattered showers and storms \nalong and ahead of the cold front.\n\nBehind the cold front, we will see drier air move into the region. \nThough, temperatures look to remain in the upper 80s for Friday. \nMoving into the weekend, ridging looks to broaden over the southern \nCONUS, which will lead to increased heights over the lower Ohio \nValley and increased temperatures into the low 90s.",
